<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Chenla</h1><p class="st-meta">550 CE – 802 CE (approx.) · capital Ishanapura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Khmer kingdom that succeeded Funan in the 6th and 7th centuries, whose temples at Sambor Prei Kuk prefigure the art of Angkor.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chenla first appears in Chinese sources as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/funan">Funan</a> in the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>, possibly centred near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-phu">Wat Phu</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>. In the late 6th century its kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhavavarman-i">Bhavavarman I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendravarman-chenla">Chitrasena</a>, who took the name Mahendravarman, attacked Funan, and Ishanavarman I completed the takeover and built a capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambor-prei-kuk">Ishanapura</a>, now Sambor Prei Kuk.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chenla's kings ruled through a network of local lords and temple foundations recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-khmer">Old Khmer</a> inscriptions, the first substantial written records of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> language. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a> dominated, with linga cults and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harihara">Harihara</a> images. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambor-prei-kuk">Sambor Prei Kuk</a>, with its octagonal brick towers, is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-i">Jayavarman I</a> around 681 Chenla split, and Chinese sources speak of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/land-chenla">Land Chenla</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/water-chenla">Water Chenla</a> from the early 8th century. In 802 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-ii">Jayavarman II</a> proclaimed himself universal monarch on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-kulen">Kulen hills</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chenla.jpg" alt="Chenla" width="320" height="295"><figcaption>Image: Tanakorn Srichaisuphakit · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mainland_Southeast_Asia_in_700_CE_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>550 CE – 802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ishanapura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bhavavarman I, Mahendravarman, Ishanavarman I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism (Shaivism), Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Khmer, Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>, <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>, <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zhenla, Kambuja, Chân Lạp</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=676&amp;focus=chenla" aria-label="Chenla on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7566 1747 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chenla lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7886 2003L7846 1933"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7846 1933h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7886 2003h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7886 2003h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=676&amp;focus=chenla">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
